# Case Analysis: Secretary for Justice v Chan Chi Wan Stephen [2017] HKCFA 15

## 1. Summary of Facts

Stephen Chan Chi Wan ("Chan") was employed by Television Broadcasts Ltd ("TVB") as General Manager (Broadcasting) under an administrative contract. Although not required to appear on camera, he voluntarily hosted a popular talk show called "Be My Guest" (志雲飯局) on a TVB pay channel, receiving no additional pay for doing so. Over 150 episodes were aired, and Chan acquired celebrity status, leading to outside engagements arranged through his agent, Tseng Pei Kun ("Tseng"), sole director of Idea Empire Advertising & Production Company Limited ("IEAP").

Olympian City ("OC"), a shopping mall, had for years sponsored TVB's New Year's Eve Countdown show. For the 2009/2010 Countdown, OC and TVB agreed on a $1.3 million sponsorship fee. It was then decided to add a live, staged edition of "Be My Guest" as an ancillary segment. OC knew TVB would not supply its "top-tier artistes" for the event, so OC independently engaged Chan through IEAP for $160,000, of which $112,000 went to Chan and $20,000 to guest Lai Yiu Cheung, with IEAP retaining $28,000. Chan and Lai wore OC promotional stickers (unlike TVB-supplied artistes). Chan did not seek TVB's permission for the outside engagement, despite a contractual obligation to do so, but TVB knew of his participation since it produced and broadcast the show. The show went ahead on 31 December 2009 and all parties were paid.

Chan and Tseng were charged with conspiracy for an agent to accept an advantage contrary to section 9(1)(a) of the Prevention of Bribery Ordinance ("POBO"), with alternative substantive charges against each. The trial judge (Judge Poon) acquitted them, holding Chan's appearance was mere "moonlighting" as a celebrity, not aimed at TVB. The prosecution appealed twice by way of case stated; the Court of Appeal twice reversed the acquittals, ultimately directing convictions on the conspiracy charge (Chan fined $84,000; Tseng fined $28,000). The appellants obtained leave to appeal to the Court of Final Appeal on certified questions concerning the mens rea required under section 9, the meaning of "in relation to the principal's affairs or business" per *Ch'ng Poh*, and the approach to the defence of reasonable excuse.

## 2. Summary of the Parties' Arguments

**The Appellants (Chan and Tseng)** argued that:
- Chan's appearance on the show was an act of "moonlighting" in his capacity as a celebrity/artiste, not as TVB's agent, and was not an act "in relation to his principal's affairs or business" within the meaning of section 9. Following *Ch'ng Poh*, the phrase is restrictive: the act must be "aimed at" and intended to "influence or affect" the principal's affairs. Chan's performance was not aimed at TVB.
- The conduct was beneficial to TVB (boosting ratings at no cost), involved no conflict of interest, no secret profit, and no use of his office; the only "wrong" was a procedural failure to obtain contractual approval for outside work.
- In any event, Chan had a reasonable excuse: TVB knew he would appear as OC's guest and must be taken to have consented to his being paid; the defence of reasonable excuse should avail him.
- On the conspiracy charge specifically, section 24 of POBO (reverse onus on defences) does not apply to conspiracy, which is an offence under the Crimes Ordinance, not the POBO; the prosecution therefore had to negate reasonable excuse, which it failed to do.

**The Respondent (Secretary for Justice)** argued that:
- Chan's performance plainly "related to TVB's affairs or business": it was part of a TVB-produced and broadcast programme, TVB approved its inclusion, and had Chan refused, TVB's ratings, programming and contractual relationship with OC would have been affected. *Ch'ng Poh* did not require the influence or effect to be adverse, nor that the act be "aimed at" the principal in any negative sense.
- There is no requirement of prejudice to the principal; the absence of detriment to TVB was irrelevant.
- Chan's senior position as General Manager meant his acts were "certainly related to TVB's business."
- On reasonable excuse, the defence was unavailable or not made out; and via POBO section 12A(1), section 24's reverse onus applied to the conspiracy charge, so the burden lay on the accused.

## 3. Legal Principles (Ratio Decidendi)

The Court of Final Appeal unanimously allowed the appeals and quashed the convictions, but by **two distinct routes**: the majority (Ribeiro PJ, with Fok PJ, Stock NPJ and Lord Walker NPJ concurring) held that the conduct did not fall within section 9 at all; Tang PJ held that although the act did relate to TVB's affairs, Chan had a reasonable excuse. The judgment articulates several important principles.

**(a) Section 9 requires proof of mens rea adapted to each variant of the offence.** Section 9 contains multiple variants: solicitation or acceptance by an agent (s 9(1)), and offering by a third party (s 9(2)); and within each, the advantage may be an inducement for a future act or a reward for a past act. The mens rea adheres to each essential actus reus ingredient in appropriately adapted form, and liability must be assessed independently according to each participant's own mens rea. Thus, an agent who accepts an advantage believing it to be a corrupt reward may be guilty even if the provider acted innocently. For offering/solicitation cases, the prosecution must prove the accused intended the advantage be accepted as an inducement or reward for an act in relation to the principal's affairs; for acceptance cases, the prosecution must prove the agent knew or believed the advantage was provided as such an inducement or reward.

**(b) The restrictive meaning of "in relation to his principal's affairs or business" — reaffirming and clarifying *Ch'ng Poh*.** The majority held that the Court of Appeal had misconstrued both section 9 and *Ch'ng Poh*. Lord Lloyd's dicta in *Ch'ng Poh* assign a **restrictive function** to the phrase: it is not enough that the recipient of the bribe is an agent in fact; the agent's act or forbearance must be **"aimed at"** and intended to **"influence or affect"** the principal's affairs or business. The Court of Appeal erred in treating the phrase as satisfied merely because Chan's show "related to TVB programmes" or because TVB's business would have been affected had Chan refused to perform. Ribeiro PJ pointedly observed that the Court of Appeal's reasoning "appears to turn the policy of section 9 on its head": it convicted Chan because TVB would have been prejudiced if he had *not* acted in the beneficial way he was induced to act. Nor was it sufficient, as Cheung JA had suggested, simply to point to Chan's managerial position; the required nexus had to be examined directly. The majority also rejected the Court of Appeal's attempt to confine *Ch'ng Poh* to the offeror's intention: Lord Lloyd's explication of the restrictive phrase applies to all variants of the offence, since the same phrase appears in both limbs.

**(c) The novel and most controversial principle: the induced conduct must be adverse to the principal's interests.** This is the most significant and potentially controversial holding. Ribeiro PJ held that, on a proper construction of section 9 in light of its mischief, the conduct "aimed at the principal's business" must be conduct which **"subverts the integrity of the agency relationship to the detriment of the principal's interests."** It is not the legislative intent to stigmatize as criminal conduct that is beneficial to and congruent with the principal's interests. Importantly, the detriment need not be immediate or tangible economic loss; it suffices that the transaction is of such a nature as to undermine the relationship of trust and loyalty the principal is entitled to expect — for example, reputational damage from a manager taking gifts for promotions, an employee selling confidential tender information, or queue-jumping for a bribe. This draws on modern employment-law recognition of the implied duty of trust and confidence. The majority distinguished *Fung Hok Cheung* (the jockey-tips case) as involving surreptitious disclosure of confidential information potentially damaging the trainer's reputation — not authority for liability absent any potentially adverse impact. This element of "adverseness" or detriment to the integrity of the agency relationship is a gloss on the statutory language that goes beyond the bare words of *Ch'ng Poh* and is the judgment's most distinctive contribution.

**(d) The mischief and structure of section 9.** The majority grounded its construction in the Ordinance's purpose (suppression of bribery and corruption) and the section's structure: the offence targets unsanctioned inducements offered to agents in relation to their principals' affairs — situations implicitly involving deviation from normal duties and undermining of the agency relationship, as reflected in the "favour or disfavour" limb. The removal of the word "corruptly" from the predecessor provision did not change the mischief; rather, the section creates offences whose defined conditions implicitly establish a corrupt transaction, subject to the defences of lawful authority and reasonable excuse.

**(e) Tang PJ's differing approach: no requirement of adversity, but a broad reading of "in relation to."** In a significant dissent on reasoning (though not on outcome), Tang PJ held that the phrase "in relation to his principal's affairs or business" falls to be **widely construed** (citing *DPP v Morgan* and *R v Majeed*), and that Chan's performance — part of a TVB-produced and televised programme — did relate to TVB's affairs. He rejected the requirement of prejudice to the principal: any advantage solicited or accepted without the principal's knowledge undermines the integrity of the agency relationship, just as under section 4 a public servant (e.g., a postman accepting a tip) commits an offence regardless of prejudice. He invoked the fiduciary principle (*Parker v McKenna*) that an agent may not profit from his agency without the principal's knowledge and consent, irrespective of actual injury, and saw no reason why the criminal law should be narrower than the civil law. On this view, the absence of "corruptly" meant the prosecution need prove only a payment for a forbidden purpose, leaving reasonable excuse as the safeguard.

**(f) The defence of reasonable excuse.** The majority held that since the prosecution had failed to establish conduct falling within section 9 at all, there was no need to assess reasonable excuse — it is not meaningful to assess the reasonableness of excusing a state of affairs that never came into existence. Tang PJ, however, found the defence established: reasonable excuse is wider than the statutory permission defence in section 9(4)–(5), and an agent's honest belief that his principal would not object — amounting in substance to consent — is the paradigm of a reasonable excuse. On the facts, TVB knew Chan would appear as OC's guest and must be taken to have consented to his being paid; the exact amount was of no consequence to TVB.

**(g) Conspiracy and the reverse onus in section 24.** The Court held (Ribeiro PJ, with Tang PJ agreeing) that **section 24 of POBO does not apply to conspiracy charges**. Section 24 on its face applies only to "proceedings... for an offence under this Ordinance," and conspiracy to commit a section 9 offence is not such an offence — it is an offence under section 159A of the Crimes Ordinance (or at common law). Nor does POBO section 12A(1) (which extends "rules of evidence" for proving substantive offences to conspiracy prosecutions) capture section 24, because section 24 stipulates how *defences* are to be established and is not concerned with the proof of an offence. Consistently with *HKSAR v Ng Po On* and *HKSAR v Lam Yuk Fai*, the prosecution must, in the normal way, prove that pursuit of the agreed course of conduct would necessarily involve commission of the section 9 offence without lawful authority or reasonable excuse. This is an important procedural principle: reverse onus provisions in the substantive offence do not attach to inchoate offences, and the prosecution bears the burden of negativing reasonable excuse in conspiracy cases.

## 4. Something Striking or Remarkable

Several features of this case stand out:

**The celebrity dimension and the irony of the facts.** The case involved one of Hong Kong's most famous television personalities — Chan was known as "TVB's god" (電視城教父) and his show "志雲飯局" (literally "Chi Wan's Dinner Party") was famous for celebrity interviews over dinner. The extraordinary irony is that Chan was prosecuted for corruption for doing something that **benefited his employer**: he hosted, at TVB's own event, the very show that made him famous, was paid by the sponsor rather than TVB, and boosted TVB's ratings at no cost to the station. As Ribeiro PJ remarked, the Court of Appeal's reasoning would have convicted him *because* TVB would have suffered if he had refused to perform — a proposition that "turns the policy of section 9 on its head." A celebrated broadcaster was fined and branded a criminal for essentially giving his employer free star power.

**A deeply fractured court on the reasoning.** Although the outcome was unanimous, the Court was sharply divided on the legal route: the majority required conduct adverse to the principal's interests (a gloss not found in the statutory text), while Tang PJ expressly rejected that requirement, adopting a much broader reading of section 9 and resting acquittal on reasonable excuse. Fok PJ felt compelled to write separately to explain why he preferred the majority's reasoning over Tang PJ's. The disagreement is not academic: it goes to the heart of how aggressively Hong Kong's anti-corruption regime applies to the private sector — a question of real consequence in a jurisdiction whose identity is built on clean governance.

**The procedural saga.** The case took an unusually tortuous path: acquittal at trial, prosecution appeal allowed, acquittal again on the reasonable-excuse issue, prosecution appeal allowed again with convictions directed by the Court of Appeal, and finally quashed by the CFA — six years of litigation over a $112,000 appearance fee. The case also produced a significant procedural ruling that section 24's reverse onus does not apply to conspiracy, meaning the prosecution must negate reasonable excuse in conspiracy cases — a point that had been left open in *Ng Po On*.

**The legislative history revelation.** The judgment unearthed a striking piece of legislative history: the word "corruptly" was deliberately deleted from the 1970 Ordinance because proving corrupt motive had caused acquittals — with the Attorney-General boldly claiming "the Crown will need to prove only a payment." The majority's insistence on an adversity/detriment element can be seen as the common law's corrective to that over-broad statutory drafting, effectively re-importing a qualitative corruption element through construction — precisely what Tang PJ declined to do. The case is thus a fascinating study in how courts police the boundary of criminal liability when the legislature, in pursuit of enforcement efficiency, drafts an offence in strikingly wide terms. [end of text]


